{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh82tFokftyoNDRNYRZn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Karate Champon**, a point-sparring karate game inspired by Karate Champ, following the plan. The single file `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183848.html` (about 1.5 MB, no external requests) and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root, and all dev files are in the matching subfolder. The full test command (`node tests/run.mjs` in the dev folder) passes 427 of 427 checks. Those include a scripted playthrough of the whole story and all four of the AI Hub harness's own runs. A search for TODO, FIXME and placeholder text in the game source found nothing; the only hit in the bundle is a comment inside the engine itself.\n\n**What's in it:**\n- **Fighting:** a twin-stick system with 15 scoring techniques plus block, jump, crouch, somersaults and a back roll. The left stick (or WASD) sets your stance and the right stick (or IJKL, or the face buttons) throws the technique. A referee with two corner judges calls every point. Points depend on timing and distance, hits during the opponent's move score double, and simultaneous hits, time-outs and judges' decisions are all in.\n- **Story:** the Champon Road has 9 matches from white belt to Champ, 3 bonus stages (break the boards, flying pots, a charging bull), 3 drills where Senpai demonstrates and you copy, cutscenes with portraits, promotions, continue, game over, an ending and high-score initials.\n- **Other modes:**\n  - Versus against a second player or the CPU at levels 1–10.\n  - Kachinuki (\"winner stays on\") for 3–4 local players.\n  - Online play in AI Hub sessions for 2–4 players.\n  - The Dojo, standalone bonus games, records and options.\n- **Attract mode:** after 10 seconds idle on any menu, a demo plays a scripted bout, then the bonus stages, a how-to and the high scores. Any input returns to the title.\n- **Dev mode:** reached from the pause menu, it has live sliders with a reset, debug toggles, warps to any part of the game, and a Technique Lab that measures each move's real reach.\n- **Controls and screens:** prompts follow the device you used last, with PlayStation symbols by default and an Xbox option. Touch-only devices get the \"Controller or keyboard required\" panel and the attract mode still runs.\n\n**What I simplified compared with the plan:**\n- **Hit detection** uses each technique's reach and height from the data table rather than boxes taken from the animated skeleton. Results never depend on animation, and the Lab confirms every ground technique lands within 4 units of the table.\n- **Online guests** get sound feedback when they press a technique, not a predicted wind-up animation.\n- **Pause in online sessions:** the menu doesn't open during a session; the plan's \"live pause\" engine change was skipped.\n- **Balance:** I changed several techniques after CPU-vs-CPU testing, because lunge punches and the jumping side kick were winning most points. The lunges reach shorter, and a sloppy jump kick now scores a half point.\n\n**Engine changes:** I made three, all recorded in `engine/CHANGES.md` in the dev folder:\n1. The right stick can now be bound to actions.\n2. A game can set its own defaults for the standard settings (used here for PlayStation symbols).\n3. Voices get their own volume channel and slider.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:38:48\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade by Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a copy-the-master drill; 2D side view on a single arena (optional Tatami 2.5D camera)\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, with the three changes above), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's own HD characters drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240, white-gi vs red-gi fighters on 8 Japanese stages and 3 bonus locations\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural character rig plus new karate poses, spins, somersaults, blocks, bows, referee and judge signals, and a code-built bull\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code in Japanese pentatonic scales, synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices plus a crowd\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:** headless suites driving `game.advance`/`game.debug` (including a full story playthrough and a fake hub with several game copies), browser checks with Playwright, and the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; host leaves; server), all behind one command\n- **Research:** the research summarized in PLAN.md (Wikipedia, TASVideos, Shacknews, arcade-history); no new research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 17: scene flags that carried over between visits, a deadlock when fighters had crossed, an impossible reverse-punch drill, a Pots Ahoy! crash, lost buffered presses, CPU blocks that were always too late, the jump-kick and lunge imbalance, the attract demo's timing, a missing storage fallback, characters the font lacks, and several layout overlaps.\n2. **How many would be critical / game breaking?** 4:\n   - Fighters who crossed over couldn't walk back to their marks, so the bout froze.\n   - Every cutscene after the first never ended, which blocked the story.\n   - The reverse-punch drill couldn't be passed, which also blocked the story.\n   - Pots Ahoy! crashed when the hero blocked.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted full playthrough of the Champon Road. It found three of the four game-breaking bugs.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The early CPU-vs-CPU balance tables. They mostly measured an AI bug (late blocks) rather than balance, and had to be rerun after each fix.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The procedural character rig. A fully dressed 3D fighter comes from a few options, and I could hook in after it poses to add bows, blocks, somersaults and referee signals.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, several times:\n   - The side view is 1.5 px per world unit, not 1.\n   - The right stick couldn't be bound to actions.\n   - Scenes keep their fields between visits.\n   - Saved data simply doesn't persist when there's no browser storage.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The point-sparring rules (referee phases, scoring, simultaneous hits, judges' decisions) and the twin-stick technique system. The engine's fight module is built around health bars, so I couldn't reuse it.\n4. **What was the hardest part?** Making karate moves read in side view: spins as a turning facing angle, back kicks aimed behind the body, and somersaults by rotating every joint after the pose is solved.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n   - Right-stick bindings, so the right stick can be the technique stick.\n   - Game-level defaults for the standard settings, so button symbols default to PlayStation.\n   - A separate voice volume channel for the gibberish voices.\n6. **If you could change one thing?** Let the right stick, and any analog axis, be bound as actions out of the box, with its own button symbols.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** It asks for a complete, testable vertical slice with the real infrastructure (attract mode, dev sliders, online, 10-foot UX), and the plan turned a 1984 arcade game into a clear, data-driven design.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan used the file names and date of a different run (max effort, 17:47:43) instead of TASK.md's. TASK.md wins, but the clash had to be noticed. The plan's scope (online prediction, the live-pause engine change, motion-capture fallbacks) is also larger than a medium-effort build can follow line by line.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Balancing the CPU to be beatable but not passive, while keeping story, versus, Kachinuki, online and attract all on one rules engine.\n4. **What would you change?** Give the plan this run's own file names and date, and mark which items are must-have versus optional polish."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1000,"cache_read_input_tokens":527252,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1000},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21963457-337f-4dbb-ac8d-3bb95e6cc630","uuid":"ff6d75a3-146a-4d7f-982d-f6a7f13c59b5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T02:46:18.664Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh82swxDNyGshcDgTVrU"}
