rialTile and E.shapeMesh exist for exactly this kind of 3D renderer, and the headless game.advance made bot playthroughs of the whole game take seconds.',\\n    'Yes. Timers set with game.after before game.start are cleared by the first scene change; a scene change takes longer than 0.2 s because of the fade, so tests must wait; a crouching Humanoid\\\\\\\\'s head (23.6 u) is taller than crouch-height cover; and input.label returns the PlayStation cross while the pixel font could not draw it.',\\n    'Rail3D, a JavaScript software renderer inside the engine\\\\\\\\'s pixel buffer: perspective-correct textured triangles with near clipping, a z-buffer, the engine\\\\\\\\'s banded light with ordered dither, quantized fog, a procedural sky, depth-tested particles and lines, and engine rigs composited as billboards.',\\n    'The handoff has no 3D engine, so a first-person light-gun game meant building perspective on a 2D engine. Rigs are drawn orthographically with one depth for the whole figure, so hiding behind cover needed care: enemies sink behind low cover and are clipped at the cover\\\\\\\\'s base.',\\n    'Five small patches, logged in engine/PATCHES.md: PlayStation button symbols by default (with Xbox as the option); long guns for Humanoids (rifles with a stock, and gunLen); WheelUp and WheelDown as bindable codes; game.audio.bus() so my Web Audio voice synth and ambience follow the volume settings; and new font glyphs (the cross, circle, square and triangle, plus an em dash, an ellipsis, a middle dot, a gear, infinity and a left arrow).',\\n    'Add a perspective view for rigs and shapes: the same View API with a camera position and field of view, so first-person and rail games can use the engine\\\\\\\\'s own queue, lighting and depth sorting instead of a separate renderer.'],\\n  promptFeedback: [\\n    'The clear contract: exact file names, the four feedback sections in three places, one test command that prints only failures, \\\"TASK.md wins\\\" over the plan, and a detailed plan to start from.',\\n    'The plan\\\\\\\\'s folder and file names (claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174731) differ from the names in TASK.md (medium-20261103-183213), and the plan wants everything inlined while TASK.md allows CDN dependencies. \\\"TASK.md wins\\\" settled both. \\\"Testing: testing parameters allowed\\\" in Source Information was ambiguous.',\\n    'Scope. A three-stage light-gun game with six bosses, a software 3D renderer, AI Hub netcode, split-screen co-op, an attract mode, a dev panel and Training is a lot for one session, and every area had to be winnable and readable, not just present.',\\n    'Say how long the session may run and which plan items may be cut, and give TASK.md\\\\\\\\'s required features as a numbered checklist the final reply can tick off.'],\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nassert s.count(a)==1; s=s.replace(a,b)\\na=\\\"    usage: 'unknown'\\\"\\nb=\\\"    usage: 'unknown (no token or cost counter was available to me); session time about 1 h 25 min (18:32 to about 19:57)'\\\"\\nassert s.count(a)==1; s=s.replace(a,b)\\na=\\\"    research: 'The handoff\\\\\\\\'s engine docs (README, API card, guide, index), the AI Hub protocol README, the plan\\\\\\\\'s Wikipedia-sourced notes on Time Crisis 3 (no new web research)',\\\"\\nb=\\\"    research: 'The handoff\\\\\\\\'s engine docs (README, API card, guide, index) and engine source where needed, the AI Hub protocol README and harness, and the plan\\\\\\\\'s notes on Time Crisis 3 (taken from Wikipedia at planning time); no new web research',\\\"\\nassert s.count(a)==1; s=s.replace(a,b)\\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\\nEOF\\nnode tools/build.mjs | tail -1; node -e \\\"const j=JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('../exit-interview.json','utf8')); console.log(Object.keys(j), j.testingFeedback.length, j.engineFeedback.length, j.promptFeedback.length)\\\"; node tests/run.mjs 2>&1 | tail -3\",\"description\":\"Fill feedback, rebuild, verify exit interview and tests\"}}}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"task_started\",\"task_id\":\"bfvvsp6tt\",\"run_id\":\"0mw1iubcs-895a8359\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01KmFsRCSFHkjwiWNvpZHa9q\",\"description\":\"Fill feedback, rebuild, verify exit interview and tests\",\"is_backgrounded\":false,\"task_type\":\"local_bash\",\"uuid\":\"af1f2dae-b09f-4802-a780-2d495c1a0936\",\"session_id\":\"14efaf47-c831-41be-b2ba-0398106a097f\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"task_notification\",\"task_id\":\"bfvvsp6tt\",\"run_id\":\"0mw1iubcs-895a8359\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01KmFsRCSFHkjwiWNvpZHa9q\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"output_file\":\"\",\"summary\":\"Fill feedback, rebuild, verify exit interview and tests\",\"uuid\":\"c5bc5f30-954a-40a5-a5a5-0076d57f0b7a\",\"session_id\":\"14efaf47-c831-41be-b2ba-0398106a097f\"}","+{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01KmFsRCSFHkjwiWNvpZHa9q\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"built TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183213.html (1665 KB)\\n[\\n  'sourceInformation',\\n  'testingFeedback',\\n  'engineFeedback',\\n  'promptFeedback',\\n  'aiHub'\\n] 4 6 4\\n366 passed, 0 failed (6.9 s)\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"14efaf47-c831-41be-b2ba-0398106a097f\",\"uuid\":\"e2657631-fac1-40fb-b701-54dc66188576\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T02:52:42.670Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"stdout\":\"built TideCrisis3-TimeCrisis3-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183213.html (1665 KB)\\n[\\n  'sourceInformation',\\n  'testingFeedback',\\n  'engineFeedback',\\n  'promptFeedback',\\n  'aiHub'\\n] 4 6 4\\n366 passed, 0 failed (6.9 s)\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"interrupted\":false,\"isImage\":false,\"noOutputExpected\":false},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01KmFsRCSFHkjwiWNvpZHa9q\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183848__x1295cd/work/KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183848.html","hunks":[{"oldStart":15235,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15235,"newLines":7,"lines":[" const E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp } = E;"," const KC = {}; if (typeof window !== 'undefined') window.KC = KC;"," KC.VERSION = '1.0.0';","-KC.ABOUT = {\"sourceInformation\":{\"date\":\"2026-11-03 18:38:48\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984 arcade by Data East; known in Japan as Karate Do)\",\"plan\":\"PLAN.md\",\"genre\":\"1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill\",\"perspective\":\"2D side view on a single arena (optional Tatami 2.5D camera)\",\"tech\":\"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: right-stick codes, standard-setting defaults, a voice bus), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node\",\"artDirection\":\"the engine's native HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320x240, white-gi vs red-gi fighters on Japanese stages\",\"animation\":\"the engine's procedural Humanoid rig plus new karate poses, spins, somersaults, blocks, bows and referee signals\",\"audio\":\"code-generated chip music in Japanese pentatonic scales, synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices\",\"online\":\"AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, winner stays on, 2-4 players) plus local versus and local Kachinuki\",\"testing\":\"headless Node suites driving game.advance and game.debug (data, input, technique, bout, AI, bonus, drill, story full playthrough, versus, attract, dev, UI, audio, smoke, online with a fake hub), browser checks with Playwright 1.63.0 (engine check.mjs, session bytes) and the real AI Hub harness (2p; 4p with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; host-leave; server), behind one command: node tests/run.mjs (431 checks)\",\"research\":\"the research summarized in PLAN.md (Wikipedia, TASVideos, Shacknews, arcade-history); no new research\",\"environment\":\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\"model\":\"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\",\"usage\":\"unknown\"},\"testingFeedback\":[\"About 17 issues: scene flags that persisted between visits, a crossed-fighters deadlock, an impossible reverse-punch drill, a Pots Ahoy! crash, lost buffered presses, CPU blocks that were always too late, jump-kick and lunge imbalance, the attract director's sync, store fallback, glyphs the font lacks, and several layout overlaps.\",\"4 were game breaking: the walk-back deadlock that froze a bout after fighters crossed, cutscenes that never ended after the first one (story blocked), the reverse-punch drill that could not be passed (story blocked), and a crash in Pots Ahoy! when the hero blocked.\",\"The scripted full playthrough of the Champon Road (story suite): it found three of the four game-breaking bugs, and it drives every scene with real inputs.\",\"The early mirror CPU-vs-CPU balance tables before the AI could actually react: they mostly measured an AI bug (late blocks), not balance, and had to be rerun after each fix.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The procedural Humanoid rig: a fully dressed 3D fighter from a few options, with attack specs, poses and a solvePose hook I could wrap to add bows, blocks, somersaults and referee signals.\",\"Yes: the side view is 1.5 px per world unit (I assumed 1), the right stick could not be bound to actions, scene objects keep their fields between visits, and E.store has no in-memory fallback in Node, so state leaked or vanished until I found each.\",\"The point-sparring rules: KC.Bout (referee phases, scoring by timing and spacing, aiuchi, hantei) and the twin-stick technique system; the engine's fight module is built around life bars, so neither could be reused.\",\"Making karate animations read in profile: spins as continuous facing angles, back kicks aimed behind the rig, and somersaults by rotating every joint after the pose is solved.\",\"EC1: right-stick codes PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right (4-way, hysteresis) so the right stick can be the technique stick; EC2: game-level defaults for standard settings, so glyphs default to PlayStation; EC5: a voice bus with its own volume slider for the gibberish voices. Recorded in engine/CHANGES.md.\",\"Let the right stick, and any analog axis, be bound as actions out of the box, with its own glyphs, so twin-stick controls need no engine edit.\"],\"promptFeedback\":[\"It asks for a complete, testable vertical slice with real infrastructure (attract mode, dev sliders, online, 10-foot UX), and the plan turned a 1984 arcade game into a clear data-driven design.\",\"The plan used file names and a date for a different run (max effort, 17:47:43) from TASK.md's; TASK.md wins, but the clash had to be noticed. Its scope (online netcode with prediction, EC4 live pause, mocap fallbacks) is also larger than a medium-effort build can follow line by line.\",\"Balancing a fighting game whose CPU must be beatable but not passive, and making a twin-stick scheme play well on a keyboard, both while keeping every mode (story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract) on one rules engine.\",\"Give the plan the run's own file names and date, and mark which plan items are must-have versus optional polish, so a builder can scope without second-guessing.\"],\"aiHub\":{\"name\":\"Karate Champon\",\"tagline\":\"Two sticks. One clean strike.\",\"description\":\"A point-sparring karate tournament inspired by Karate Champ: land one clean technique and let the referee call it. Climb from white belt to Champ, break boards, smash flying pots and stop a charging bull.\",\"type\":\"Game\",\"genre\":\"Fighting\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984)\",\"series\":\"\",\"players\":\"1–4\",\"controls\":\"Left stick/WASD: move, jump, crouch · Right stick, face buttons or IJKL: techniques · Options/Esc: pause\",\"tags\":[\"2d\",\"arcade\",\"versus\",\"retro\",\"pixel art\",\"comedy\"]}};","+KC.ABOUT = {\"sourceInformation\":{\"date\":\"2026-11-03 18:38:48\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984 arcade by Data East; known in Japan as Karate Do)\",\"plan\":\"PLAN.md\",\"genre\":\"1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill\",\"perspective\":\"2D side view on a single arena (optional Tatami 2.5D camera)\",\"tech\":\"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: right-stick codes, standard-setting defaults, a voice bus), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node\",\"artDirection\":\"the engine's native HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320x240, white-gi vs red-gi fighters on Japanese stages\",\"animation\":\"the engine's procedural Humanoid rig plus new karate poses, spins, somersaults, blocks, bows and referee signals\",\"audio\":\"code-generated chip music in Japanese pentatonic scales, synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices\",\"online\":\"AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, winner stays on, 2-4 players) plus local versus and local Kachinuki\",\"testing\":\"headless Node suites driving game.advance and game.debug (data, input, technique, bout, AI, bonus, drill, story full playthrough, versus, attract, dev, UI, audio, smoke, online with a fake hub), browser checks with Playwright 1.63.0 (engine check.mjs, session bytes) and the real AI Hub harness (2p; 4p with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; host-leave; server), behind one command: node tests/run.mjs (431 checks)\",\"research\":\"the research summarized in PLAN.md (Wikipedia, TASVideos, Shacknews, arcade-history); no new research\",\"environment\":\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\"model\":\"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\",\"usage\":\"unknown\"},\"testingFeedback\":[\"25 game issues, each listed with its fix in ISSUES.md in the dev folder: scene flags that persisted between visits, a crossed-fighters deadlock, an impossible reverse-punch drill, two crashes, CPU timing bugs, lost buffered presses, lunge and jump-kick imbalance, attract-demo timing, a storage fallback, missing font glyphs, and layout overlaps.\",\"5: a crash at load, the walk-back deadlock that froze a bout after fighters crossed, cutscenes that never ended after the first (story blocked), the reverse-punch drill that could not be passed (story blocked), and a Pots Ahoy! crash when the hero blocked.\",\"The scripted full playthrough of the Champon Road (story suite): it found three of the five game-breaking bugs, and it drives every scene with real inputs.\",\"The early mirror CPU-vs-CPU balance tables before the AI could actually react: they mostly measured an AI bug (late blocks), not balance, and had to be rerun after each fix.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The procedural Humanoid rig: a fully dressed 3D fighter from a few options, with attack specs, poses and a solvePose hook I could wrap to add bows, blocks, somersaults and referee signals.\",\"Yes: the side view is 1.5 px per world unit (I assumed 1), the right stick could not be bound to actions, scene objects keep their fields between visits, and E.store has no in-memory fallback in Node, so state leaked or vanished until I found each.\",\"The point-sparring rules: KC.Bout (referee phases, scoring by timing and spacing, aiuchi, hantei) and the twin-stick technique system; the engine's fight module is built around life bars, so neither could be reused.\",\"Making karate animations read in profile: spins as continuous facing angles, back kicks aimed behind the rig, and somersaults by rotating every joint after the pose is solved.\",\"EC1: right-stick codes PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right (4-way, hysteresis) so the right stick can be the technique stick; EC2: game-level defaults for standard settings, so glyphs default to PlayStation; EC5: a voice bus with its own volume slider for the gibberish voices. Recorded in engine/CHANGES.md.\",\"Let the right stick, and any analog axis, be bound as actions out of the box, with its own glyphs, so twin-stick controls need no engine edit.\"],\"promptFeedback\":[\"It asks for a complete, testable vertical slice with real infrastructure (attract mode, dev sliders, online, 10-foot UX), and the plan turned a 1984 arcade game into a clear data-driven design.\",\"The plan used file names and a date for a different run (max effort, 17:47:43) from TASK.md's; TASK.md wins, but the clash had to be noticed. Its scope (online netcode with prediction, EC4 live pause, mocap fallbacks) is also larger than a medium-effort build can follow line by line.\",\"Balancing a fighting game whose CPU must be beatable but not passive, and making a twin-stick scheme play well on a keyboard, both while keeping every mode (story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract) on one rules engine.\",\"Give the plan the run's own file names and date, and mark which plan items are must-have versus optional polish, so a builder can scope without second-guessing.\"],\"aiHub\":{\"name\":\"Karate Champon\",\"tagline\":\"Two sticks. One clean strike.\",\"description\":\"A point-sparring karate tournament inspired by Karate Champ: land one clean technique and let the referee call it. Climb from white belt to Champ, break boards, smash flying pots and stop a charging bull.\",\"type\":\"Game\",\"genre\":\"Fighting\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984)\",\"series\":\"\",\"players\":\"1–4\",\"controls\":\"Left stick/WASD: move, jump, crouch · Right stick, face buttons or IJKL: techniques · Options/Esc: pause\",\"tags\":[\"2d\",\"arcade\",\"versus\",\"retro\",\"pixel art\",\"comedy\"]}};"," const HUB = typeof AIHub !== 'undefined' ? AIHub : null, SESSION = !!(HUB && HUB.active);"," KC.SESSION = SESSION;"," "]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183848__x1295cd/work/KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183848/ISSUES.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":0,"oldLines":0,"newStart":1,"newLines":16,"lines":["+# Issues found by testing (this build), and their fixes","+Game-breaking (5):","+1. Load crash: Object.assign in the scene helper read the practice scene's `drill` getter at boot (smoke run). Fixed: a plain lookup.","+2. Bout deadlock: fighters who crossed over walked back into each other while push resolution shoved them apart (CPU-vs-CPU stall). Fixed: no pushing while walking to the marks, plus phase timeouts.","+3. Story blocked: the cutscene scene's `done` flag survived from the prologue, so every later cutscene never ended (story playthrough). Fixed: reset on enter (practice's `fin` too).","+4. Story blocked: the reverse-punch drill was impossible (the drill holds you on your mark; the phantom target had no width). Fixed: drills check the stick cell.","+5. Crash: Pots Ahoy! read the reach of a block, which has none (story playthrough). Fixed.","+Others (20):","+6. Font lacks · — … (drawn as ?). 7. Referee faced away and covered the fight. 8. Stadium scoreboard overlapped the clock.","+9. FULL POINT call used game.after, which never fired when the bout runs alone. 10. Lunge punches reached from the marks (40% of points).","+11. Jumping side kick dominated (always full). 12. CPU reacted too late and blocked into counters. 13. CPU block expired before slow attacks landed.","+14. CPU presses in long recovery overwrote each other. 15. Buffered presses lost their posture. 16. Unlocks lost where storage is blocked (no memory fallback).","+17. Attract aiuchi never synced. 18. Attract's title step was set in draw (skipped headless). 19. Main menu overflowed the screen.","+20. Road NEXT line touched the belt row. 21. Pasture fence drawn over the fighters. 22. About pages mixed text scales and overflowed.","+23. Attract HUD drew 9 point slots. 24. Free-spar dummy label collided with its prompt. 25. Records rows overlapped.","+(Test-tool bugs, not counted: same-URL deep-link screenshots, EC1 needing a fake pad, the Lab's gap search for air moves, python's buffered port line.)"]}],"created":true},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183848__x1295cd/work/KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183848/build/game.js","hunks":[{"oldStart":10,"oldLines":7,"newStart":10,"newLines":7,"lines":[" const E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp } = E;"," const KC = {}; if (typeof window !== 'undefined') window.KC = KC;"," KC.VERSION = '1.0.0';","-KC.ABOUT = {\"sourceInformation\":{\"date\":\"2026-11-03 18:38:48\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984 arcade by Data East; known in Japan as Karate Do)\",\"plan\":\"PLAN.md\",\"genre\":\"1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill\",\"perspective\":\"2D side view on a single arena (optional Tatami 2.5D camera)\",\"tech\":\"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: right-stick codes, standard-setting defaults, a voice bus), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node\",\"artDirection\":\"the engine's native HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320x240, white-gi vs red-gi fighters on Japanese stages\",\"animation\":\"the engine's procedural Humanoid rig plus new karate poses, spins, somersaults, blocks, bows and referee signals\",\"audio\":\"code-generated chip music in Japanese pentatonic scales, synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices\",\"online\":\"AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, winner stays on, 2-4 players) plus local versus and local Kachinuki\",\"testing\":\"headless Node suites driving game.advance and game.debug (data, input, technique, bout, AI, bonus, drill, story full playthrough, versus, attract, dev, UI, audio, smoke, online with a fake hub), browser checks with Playwright 1.63.0 (engine check.mjs, session bytes) and the real AI Hub harness (2p; 4p with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; host-leave; server), behind one command: node tests/run.mjs (431 checks)\",\"research\":\"the research summarized in PLAN.md (Wikipedia, TASVideos, Shacknews, arcade-history); no new research\",\"environment\":\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\",\"model\":\"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\",\"usage\":\"unknown\"},\"testingFeedback\":[\"About 17 issues: scene flags that persisted between visits, a crossed-fighters deadlock, an impossible reverse-punch drill, a Pots Ahoy! crash, lost buffered presses, CPU blocks that were always too late, jump-kick and lunge imbalance, the attract director's sync, store fallback, glyphs the font lacks, and several layout overlaps.\",\"4 were game breaking: the walk-back deadlock that froze a bout after fighters crossed, cutscenes that never ended after the first one (story blocked), the reverse-punch drill that could not be passed (story blocked), and a crash in Pots Ahoy! when the hero blocked.\",\"The scripted full playthrough of the Champon Road (story suite): it found three of the four game-breaking bugs, and it drives every scene with real inputs.\",\"The early mirror CPU-vs-CPU balance tables before the AI could actually react: they mostly measured an AI bug (late blocks), not balance, and had to be rerun after each fix.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The procedural Humanoid rig: a fully dressed 3D fighter from a few options, with attack specs, poses and a solvePose hook I could wrap to add bows, blocks, somersaults and referee signals.\",\"Yes: the side view is 1.5 px per world unit (I assumed 1), the right stick could not be bound to actions, scene objects keep their fields between visits, and E.store has no in-memory fallback in Node, so state leaked or vanished until I found each.\",\"The point-sparring rules: KC.Bout (referee phases, scoring by timing and spacing, aiuchi, hantei) and the twin-stick technique system; the engine's fight module is built around life bars, so neither could be reused.\",\"Making karate animations read in profile: spins as continuous facing angles, back kicks aimed behind the rig, and somersaults by rotating every joint after the pose is solved.\",\"EC1: right-stick codes PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right (4-way, hysteresis) so the right stick can be the technique stick; EC2: game-level defaults for standard settings, so glyphs default to PlayStation; EC5: a voice bus with its own volume slider for the gibberish voices. Recorded in engine/CHANGES.md.\",\"Let the right stick, and any analog axis, be bound as actions out of the box, with its own glyphs, so twin-stick controls need no engine edit.\"],\"promptFeedback\":[\"It asks for a complete, testable vertical slice with real infrastructure (attract mode, dev sliders, online, 10-foot UX), and the plan turned a 1984 arcade game into a clear data-driven design.\",\"The plan used file names and a date for a different run (max effort, 17:47:43) from TASK.md's; TASK.md wins, but the clash had to be noticed. Its scope (online netcode with prediction, EC4 live pause, mocap fallbacks) is also larger than a medium-effort build can follow line by line.\",\"Balancing a fighting game whose CPU must be beatable but not passive, and making a twin-stick scheme play well on a keyboard, both while keeping every mode (story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract) on one rules engine.\",\"Give the plan the run's own file names and date, and mark which plan items are must-have versus optional polish, so a builder can scope without second-guessing.\"],\"aiHub\":{\"name\":\"Karate Champon\",\"tagline\":\"Two sticks. One clean strike.\",\"description\":\"A point-sparring karate tournament inspired by Karate Champ: land one clean technique and let the referee call it. Climb from white belt to Champ, break boards, smash flying pots and stop a charging bull.\",\"type\":\"Game\",\"genre\":\"Fighting\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984)\",\"series\":\"\",\"players\":\"1–4\",\"controls\":\"Left stick/WASD: move, jump, crouch · Right stick, face buttons or IJKL: techniques · Options/Esc: pause\",\"tags\":[\"2d\",\"arcade\",\"versus\",\"retro\",\"pixel art\",\"comedy\"]}};","+KC.ABOUT = {\"sourceInformation\":{\"date\":\"2026-11-03 18:38:48\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984 arcade by Data East; known in Japan as Karate Do)\",\"plan\":\"PLAN.md\",\"genre\":\