froze a bout after fighters crossed, cutscenes that never ended after the first (story blocked), the reverse-punch drill that could not be passed (story blocked), and a Pots Ahoy! crash when the hero blocked.\",\"The scripted full playthrough of the Champon Road (story suite): it found three of the five game-breaking bugs, and it drives every scene with real inputs.\",\"The early mirror CPU-vs-CPU balance tables before the AI could actually react: they mostly measured an AI bug (late blocks), not balance, and had to be rerun after each fix.\"],\"engineFeedback\":[\"The procedural Humanoid rig: a fully dressed 3D fighter from a few options, with attack specs, poses and a solvePose hook I could wrap to add bows, blocks, somersaults and referee signals.\",\"Yes: the side view is 1.5 px per world unit (I assumed 1), the right stick could not be bound to actions, scene objects keep their fields between visits, and E.store has no in-memory fallback in Node, so state leaked or vanished until I found each.\",\"The point-sparring rules: KC.Bout (referee phases, scoring by timing and spacing, aiuchi, hantei) and the twin-stick technique system; the engine's fight module is built around life bars, so neither could be reused.\",\"Making karate animations read in profile: spins as continuous facing angles, back kicks aimed behind the rig, and somersaults by rotating every joint after the pose is solved.\",\"EC1: right-stick codes PadRAxisUp/Down/Left/Right (4-way, hysteresis) so the right stick can be the technique stick; EC2: game-level defaults for standard settings, so glyphs default to PlayStation; EC5: a voice bus with its own volume slider for the gibberish voices. Recorded in engine/CHANGES.md.\",\"Let the right stick, and any analog axis, be bound as actions out of the box, with its own glyphs, so twin-stick controls need no engine edit.\"],\"promptFeedback\":[\"It asks for a complete, testable vertical slice with real infrastructure (attract mode, dev sliders, online, 10-foot UX), and the plan turned a 1984 arcade game into a clear data-driven design.\",\"The plan used file names and a date for a different run (max effort, 17:47:43) from TASK.md's; TASK.md wins, but the clash had to be noticed. Its scope (online netcode with prediction, EC4 live pause, mocap fallbacks) is also larger than a medium-effort build can follow line by line.\",\"Balancing a fighting game whose CPU must be beatable but not passive, and making a twin-stick scheme play well on a keyboard, both while keeping every mode (story, versus, Kachinuki, online, attract) on one rules engine.\",\"Give the plan the run's own file names and date, and mark which plan items are must-have versus optional polish, so a builder can scope without second-guessing.\"],\"aiHub\":{\"name\":\"Karate Champon\",\"tagline\":\"Two sticks. One clean strike.\",\"description\":\"A point-sparring karate tournament inspired by Karate Champ: land one clean technique and let the referee call it. Climb from white belt to Champ, break boards, smash flying pots and stop a charging bull.\",\"type\":\"Game\",\"genre\":\"Fighting\",\"source\":\"Karate Champ (1984)\",\"series\":\"\",\"players\":\"1–4\",\"controls\":\"Left stick/WASD: move, jump, crouch · Right stick, face buttons or IJKL: techniques · Options/Esc: pause\",\"tags\":[\"2d\",\"arcade\",\"versus\",\"retro\",\"pixel art\",\"comedy\"]}};"," const HUB = typeof AIHub !== 'undefined' ? AIHub : null, SESSION = !!(HUB && HUB.active);"," KC.SESSION = SESSION;"," "]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183848__x1295cd/work/KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183848/data/about.json","hunks":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":9,"newStart":17,"newLines":9,"lines":["     \"usage\": \"unknown\"","   },","   \"testingFeedback\": [","-    \"About 17 issues: scene flags that persisted between visits, a crossed-fighters deadlock, an impossible reverse-punch drill, a Pots Ahoy! crash, lost