test items, checked here","-const root = new URL('../../', here), html = readFileSync(new URL(NAME + '.html', root), 'utf8');","+const root = new URL('../', here), html = readFileSync(new URL(NAME + '.html', root), 'utf8');"," const dev = existsSync(new URL('src', here)) && existsSync(new URL('tests', here)) && existsSync(new URL('engine/CHANGES.md', here));"," const src = readdirSync(new URL('src/', here)).map(f => readFileSync(new URL('src/' + f, here), 'utf8')).join('\\n'), todo = /\\b(TODO|FIXME|XXX)\\b|placeholder|\\bstub\\b/i.test(src);"," const j = JSON.parse(readFileSync(new URL('exit-interview.json', root), 'utf8')), shapeOk = Object.keys(j.sourceInformation).length === 15 && j.testingFeedback.length === 4 && j.engineFeedback.length === 6 && j.promptFeedback.length === 4 && ['name', 'tagline', 'description', 'type', 'genre', 'source', 'series', 'players', 'controls', 'tags'].every(k => k in j.aiHub) && Object.values(j.sourceInformation).every(v => v);"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183848__x1295cd/work/KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183848/AUDIT.md","/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183848__x1295cd/work/KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261103-183848/tools/audit.mjs"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0142fRmaoBzCeRWCb7gYvm5K","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh9QikYgRb7JUQVAmges","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've audited TASK.md line by line: every requirement maps to at least one check in the test suites, and all of those checks passed in the last full run (431 of 431). The audit file is `AUDIT.md`, generated by `node tools/audit.mjs` (both in the dev folder). The script finds each cited check by its exact name in the test files, and it checks the remaining items directly:\n- the dev subfolder exists;\n- the source has no TODO, FIXME, XXX, placeholder or stub;\n- `exit-interview.json` parses, has the exact shape TASK.md gives, and every field is filled;\n- the bundle sits in the root.\n\n| TASK.md item | Proved by (passing checks) |\n|---|---|\n| Original game from Karate Champ 1984 | a match against every roster fighter completes; the full playthrough reaches the end of the Champon Road |\n| Genre and perspective | 5-second runs of every scene and stage; the Tatami 2.5D view |\n| Title riff | `<title>Karate Champon</title>` |\n| Single HTML file with the required name | session client inline, byte-identical and first; no external requests |\n| Built on the engine | right stick presses the technique actions; PlayStation symbols by default |\n| Art direction | every stage and bonus scene runs; screenshots reviewed |\n| Animation | front somersault crosses over; spins end facing the opponent |\n| Audio in code, gibberish voices | every song plays with no warnings; voice plans are deterministic; referee calls keep a fixed rhythm |\n| Online via AI Hub, and the game on its own | all 4 harness runs; guest input reaches the host; pause in a session keeps the bout running; the title appears outside a session |\n| Feature-complete demo | full playthrough, with every scene visited |\n| Distinctive source traits | full point scored; STOP, WHITE, FULL POINT called; perfect board break; every lesson completes |\n| Dev mode, live sliders, reset | Dev mode in the pause menu; a slider changes its value; reset all; hit-stop and full-point sliders change play; P1 invincible |\n| Attract mode | starts after 10 s idle; loops through bout, bonus, how-to, scores, title; a key or mouse move exits |\n| Multiplayer | P2 joins on the keyboard; 3+ players start Kachinuki; bouts decided online |\n| Narrative tone | English and Japanese referee calls |\n| 10-foot UX with native keyboard and mouse | menu focus moves with keys and mouse hover; a click picks |\n| Prompts follow the last device; PlayStation default, Xbox option | pad, keyboard and mouse prompts; Xbox setting |\n| No dense data screens | About paged at 8 lines a page or fewer; move list shows one posture row at a time |\n| Touch-only devices | gate shows, no touch controls, attract still runs |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | source scan by the audit script |\n| Source Information and feedback on the About screen | all 15 fields plus 4/6/4 answers checked; all 12 pages screenshotted; identical to `exit-interview.json` |\n| `exit-interview.json` | parses, exact shape, no empty fields |\n| Testing: headless, full playthrough, one command | `node tests/run.mjs` prints only failures and one count line |\n\nTASK.md doesn't prescribe how hits are detected. The game checks each technique's reach and height from its data table, and the Technique Lab check confirms every ground technique's measured reach matches that table within 4 units.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:38:48\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade by Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a copy-the-master drill; 2D side view on a single arena (optional Tatami 2.5D camera)\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, with three changes: right-stick bindings, game-level setting defaults, a voice volume channel), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's own HD characters drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240, white-gi vs red-gi fighters on 8 Japanese stages and 3 bonus locations\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural character rig plus new karate poses, spins, somersaults, blocks, bows, referee and judge signals, and a code-built bull\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code in Japanese pentatonic scales, synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices plus a crowd\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, 2–4 players, with in-session pause and guest prediction), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:**\n  - Headless suites driving `game.advance`/`game.debug`, including a full story playthrough and a fake hub running several game copies.\n  - Browser checks with Playwright.\n  - The real AI Hub harness: 2 players; 4 players with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5; host leaves; server.\n  - All behind one command, `node tests/run.mjs` (431 checks).\n- **Research:** the research summarized in PLAN.md (Wikipedia, TASVideos, Shacknews, arcade-history); no new research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time about 1 h 25 min (18:38:48 start, clock checked at 19:59:49, plus the final checks); API cost and token counts unknown (not visible to the agent in this session)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25 game issues, each listed with its fix in `ISSUES.md` in the dev folder. They include:\n   - scene flags that carried over between visits;\n   - a deadlock when fighters had crossed;\n   - an impossible reverse-punch drill;\n   - two crashes;\n   - CPU timing bugs and lost buffered presses;\n   - lunge and jump-kick imbalance;\n   - attract-demo timing;\n   - a missing storage fallback;\n   - characters the font lacks, and layout overlaps.\n2. **How many would be critical / game breaking?** 5:\n   - A crash at load.\n   - Fighters who crossed over couldn't walk back to their marks, so the bout froze.\n   - Every cutscene after the first never ended, which blocked the story.\n   - The reverse-punch drill couldn't be passed, which also blocked the story.\n   - Pots Ahoy! crashed when the hero blocked.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted full playthrough of the Champon Road. It found three of the five game-breaking bugs.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The early CPU-vs-CPU balance tables. They mostly measured an AI bug (late blocks) rather than balance, and had to be rerun after each fix.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The procedural character rig. A fully dressed 3D fighter comes from a few options, and I could hook in after it poses to add bows, blocks, somersaults and referee signals.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, several times:\n   - The side view is 1.5 px per world unit, not 1.\n   - The right stick couldn't be bound to actions.\n   - Scenes keep their fields between visits.\n   - Saved data simply doesn't persist when there's no browser storage.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The point-sparring rules (referee phases, scoring, simultaneous hits, judges' decisions) and the twin-stick technique system. The engine's fight module is built around health bars, so I couldn't reuse it.\n4. **What was the hardest part?** Making karate moves read in side view: spins as a turning facing angle, back kicks aimed behind the body, and somersaults by rotating every joint after the pose is solved.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** All recorded in `engine/CHANGES.md`:\n   - Right-stick bindings, so the right stick can be the technique stick.\n   - Game-level defaults for the standard settings, so button symbols default to PlayStation.\n   - A separate voice volume channel for the gibberish voices.\n6. **If you could change one thing?** Let the right stick, and any analog axis, be bound as actions out of the box, with its own button symbols.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** It asks for a complete, testable vertical slice with the real infrastructure (attract mode, dev sliders, online, 10-foot UX), and the plan turned a 1984 arcade game into a clear, data-driven design.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan used the file names and date of a different run (max effort, 17:47:43) instead of TASK.md's. TASK.md wins, but the clash had to be noticed. The plan's scope is also larger than a medium-effort build can follow line by line.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Balancing the CPU to be beatable but not passive, while keeping story, versus, Kachinuki, online and attract all on one rules engine.\n4. **What would you change?** Give the plan this run's own file names and date, and mark which items are must-have versus optional polish."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1367,"cache_read_input_tokens":580217,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1367},"output_tokens":707,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"21963457-337f-4dbb-ac8d-3bb95e6cc630","uuid":"36135e8c-3232-4049-b3fb-37257ee08823","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:04:19.376Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh9QiRT1XrNWehvoychz"}
